%I A398642 #12 Aug 17 2026 12:47:14
%S A398642 1,2,6,50,4085,3289853,51159900399,5058270859270837,
%T A398642 9898641380916292576410,125792767018585888240739789507
%N A398642 Number of quasi-regular graphs on 2*n + 1 vertices.
%C A398642 A quasi-regular graph is a simple graph with one vertex of degree r + 1 and all other vertices of degree r. By the handshake lemma, such a graph necessarily has odd order and odd r.
%C A398642 For graph order N, a term is the sum, over r = 1, 3, ..., N - 2, of the numbers of unlabeled graph isomorphism classes.
%C A398642 For r = 1, the unique graph of order N is the disjoint union of the path graph P_3 and (N - 3)/2 copies of K_2.
%C A398642 For N = 13, the contributions for r = 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 are 1, 1999, 1877695, 1409285, 872, 1, respectively.
%C A398642 For N = 15, the contributions for r = 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13 are 1, 22186, 1505997049, 48750377244, 903496103, 7815, 1, respectively.
%C A398642 For N = 15, every contribution with r >= 7 is connected: a disconnected graph would require a quasi-r component on at least r + 2 vertices and a nonempty r-regular component on at least r + 1 vertices, totaling at least 2*r + 3 > 15 vertices.
%H A398642 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HandshakingLemma.html">Handshaking Lemma</a>.
%H A398642 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Quasi-RegularGraph.html">Quasi-Regular Graph</a>.
%e A398642 a(7) = 51159900399 because there are 51159900399 quasi-regular graphs on 15 vertices.
%Y A398642 Row sums of A399014.
%Y A398642 Cf. A398638 (quasi-cubic graphs, r = 3).
%Y A398642 Cf. A398640 (quasi-quintic graphs, r = 5).
%Y A398642 Cf. A398643 (connected quasi-regular graphs).
